  "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.2+3\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5+0.4+0.3+0.8 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.2)-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.5+0.8+0.9+3.2)-4 \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2,\\frac{1.4}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(2,0.028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2) & ≈P(Z>\\frac{2.2-2}{\\sqrt{0.028}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.20) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2)=1-Φ(1.20).\n$$",
